WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.500
The first film followed calcium through the kidney's filter: a huge daily flow, and

2
00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:06.620
a small share lost.

3
00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:09.540
Phosphate passes the same filter.

4
00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:13.700
Picture a healthy kidney and a badly failing one.

5
00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:19.160
In round numbers, both excrete 800 mg of phosphate a day.

6
00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:22.920
So why is blood phosphate high in the failing one?

7
00:00:26.333 --> 00:00:28.133
First, phosphate's controls.

8
00:00:29.653 --> 00:00:33.073
PTH throws phosphate out, but calcium sets PTH.

9
00:00:34.453 --> 00:00:37.813
So bone makes phosphate its own brake, FGF23.

10
00:00:39.273 --> 00:00:42.093
It acts on the kidney through a co-receptor, Klotho.

11
00:00:43.017 --> 00:00:44.477
There it does two things.

12
00:00:45.737 --> 00:00:50.257
Like PTH, it lowers phosphate reabsorption in the proximal tubule.

13
00:00:51.557 --> 00:00:56.937
Unlike PTH, it lowers calcitriol, which cuts the phosphate absorbed from the gut.

14
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:00.440
Now the arithmetic, in round numbers.

15
00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:07.420
Phosphate excreted equals filtration, times filterable plasma phosphate, times the

16
00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:08.760
fraction excreted.

17
00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:13.060
A healthy filter passes 180 L a day.

18
00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:16.880
15 percent of filtered phosphate leaves.

19
00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:22.920
A plasma phosphate of about 3.3 mg/dL does the job.

20
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:30.020
Here is the rule. At steady state, the kidneys must excrete the phosphate the gut

21
00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:35.180
absorbs. When filtration falls, the fraction excreted must rise.

22
00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:39.900
Where the fraction falls short, plasma phosphate rises instead.

23
00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:43.480
Cut filtration to 60 L a day.

24
00:01:44.740 --> 00:01:48.180
At 15 phosphate would need about 9.9.

25
00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:55.460
If FGF23 and PTH push the fraction to 50 about 3.0 is enough.

26
00:01:56.533 --> 00:02:01.593
So moderate kidney disease keeps phosphate normal largely by raising the fraction.

27
00:02:03.013 --> 00:02:08.993
In one large cohort, FGF23 was high while phosphate and PTH were still normal.

28
00:02:10.193 --> 00:02:13.013
That fits it rising first, without proving the order.

29
00:02:14.117 --> 00:02:16.857
Now cut filtration to 15 L a day.

30
00:02:18.537 --> 00:02:21.177
Suppose the kidney excreted every milligram it filtered.

31
00:02:22.477 --> 00:02:24.097
Could plasma phosphate stay normal?

32
00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:38.840
No. The fraction cannot pass 100 Even there, plasma phosphate must sit near 5.9.

33
00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:43.400
At 50 it would need about 11.9.

34
00:02:44.450 --> 00:02:47.530
So phosphate settles high, and climbs from there.

35
00:02:48.850 --> 00:02:52.910
Calcitriol falls and PTH rises, yet phosphate stays up.

36
00:02:54.270 --> 00:02:58.730
PTH's usual signature, calcium up and phosphate down, fails here.

37
00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:03.400
A smaller filter can be covered by sending out a larger share.

38
00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:07.640
When the share can grow no more, the blood level has to give.
